WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:02.461
This article documents the

00:00:02.461 --> 00:00:03.521
Smart Fab Edge AI

00:00:03.521 --> 00:00:04.981
system architecture I presented

00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:06.210
in an interview presentation,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.401
expanding the original 11-slide

00:00:08.401 --> 00:00:09.501
deck into a more

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:11.630
comprehensive technical reference.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.981
The project metrics cited in

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:15.621
each section are collected in

00:00:15.621 --> 00:00:16.741
the appendix at the end,

00:00:16.741 --> 00:00:19.170
together with their measurement caveats.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.961
Starting with equipment sensor

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:22.121
data, the article walks

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:23.650
through real-time data processing,

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:27.581
anomaly detection, Remaining Useful Life

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:28.421
(RUL) prediction, edge deployment,

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:30.521
industrial knowledge retrieval, and the

00:00:30.521 --> 00:00:32.330
decision boundaries of AI agents.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.661
The question it actually tries

00:00:34.661 --> 00:00:35.961
to answer is not simply

00:00:35.961 --> 00:00:36.961
how to deploy AI

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:38.061
models in a factory,

00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:39.161
but how to keep them

00:00:39.161 --> 00:00:41.310
running under constrained resources —

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.741
trading off latency, compute, reliability,

00:00:43.741 --> 00:00:45.001
and operating cost —

00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:46.681
while integrating with existing

00:00:46.681 --> 00:00:48.950
equipment controls, maintenance workflows,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.197
and factory information systems.
